OBSERVATIONS:
  - Exercise 1 (Bcast) is by far the SLOWEST at every process count.
    Reason: MPI_Bcast sends the ENTIRE 4 MB array to EVERY process,
    consuming far more network bandwidth than necessary.

  - Exercises 2-6 are all roughly comparable in speed because they
    all use MPI_Scatter (only ~1 MB per process), which is the
    dominant cost. The difference between Gather, Reduce, Allreduce,
    and Scan is negligible for a single scalar value.

  - All programs scale well: doubling the process count roughly
    halves the time, confirming good parallel efficiency on this
    embarrassingly parallel workload.

  - Exercise 4 (Reduce) is marginally faster than Exercise 3 (Gather)
    because MPI_Reduce uses an O(log P) tree algorithm internally,
    whereas Gather + manual loop is O(P) on root.

  - Exercise 5 (Allreduce) is slightly slower than Exercise 4 (Reduce)
    because it broadcasts the result to all processes, but this cost
    is tiny for a single long long value.

WHY Bcast IS SO MUCH SLOWER:
  With np=4 and N=1,000,000 integers (4 bytes each):
    Bcast  transfers: 4,000,000 bytes × 4 processes = 16 MB total
    Scatter transfers: 1,000,000 bytes × 4 processes =  4 MB total
  Scatter sends 4× less data overall, which is the primary speedup.


---------------------------------------------------------------
PART 3: Thinking Question
---------------------------------------------------------------

Q: In what situation would you choose MPI_Scan over MPI_Allreduce?
   Give a concrete example.

A: Use MPI_Scan when each process needs to know the cumulative total
   of ALL PREVIOUS ranks, not just the global total.

   MPI_Allreduce gives every process the SAME single global answer.
   MPI_Scan gives every process a DIFFERENT answer — the running
   total from rank 0 up to and including itself.

   CONCRETE EXAMPLE — Parallel File Writing (Global Offset Assignment):

     Suppose each of P processes has generated a variable number of
     output records and you need to write them all to a single shared
     binary file without overlap.

     Each process knows its own local_count (how many records it has).
     You need to know the file byte offset at which each process should
     start writing.

     With MPI_Scan(MPI_SUM) on local_count:
       Rank 0: prefix = local_count_0              → starts at byte 0
       Rank 1: prefix = local_count_0+local_count_1 → starts after rank 0
       Rank k: prefix = sum of counts 0..k          → starts after ranks 0..k-1

     Each process computes:  my_offset = prefix_sum - local_count
     Then calls:             MPI_File_write_at(fh, my_offset, ...)

     This requires ZERO extra communication beyond the single Scan call.
     MPI_Allreduce alone cannot do this — it only gives the grand total,
     not each rank's individual starting position.

   OTHER USE CASES FOR MPI_SCAN:
     • Globally consistent array index assignment across processes
     • Computing cumulative distribution functions in parallel histogram
     • Load-balanced work partitioning where chunk sizes are unequal
     • Parallel prefix operations (e.g., parallel merge sort boundaries)

